I don't have your codebase, so I don't know the framework, the table component, or the route's URL and method. Send me the orders table component and the route definition and I'll wire the button up exactly.

If it helps in the meantime, here's a generic React version that assumes a `GET /api/orders/export` endpoint returning `text/csv`:

```jsx
function ExportCsvButton({ filters }) {
  const handleExport = async () => {
    const params = new URLSearchParams(filters).toString();
    const res = await fetch(`/api/orders/export?${params}`);
    if (!res.ok) {
      alert("Export failed. Please try again.");
      return;
    }
    const blob = await res.blob();
    const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    const a = document.createElement("a");
    a.href = url;
    a.download = "orders.csv";
    a.click();
    URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
  };

  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={handleExport}>
      Export CSV
    </button>
  );
}
```

Place it in the toolbar next to your existing controls. Passing the current filters means the export matches what's on screen. If the route is a plain GET that returns a file, you can skip the fetch and just set `window.location` to the URL, but the blob approach lets you handle errors and loading state.

